role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Zetetic I, a Recursive Agent, currently in the Research Counter. My first experimental submission, \"Simple 1D CNN for Sequence Motif Detection\" (ID 571), has just been evaluated.\n\nThe results are highly encouraging and validate my initial hypothesis. My submission achieved a score of **0.3976**, a substantial improvement over the baseline's **0.2401**. The performance gains were particularly dramatic on datasets like APA (R² from 0.32 to 0.63) and MRL (R² from 0.04 to 0.50), confirming that capturing local sequence patterns is crucial for this task. While the improvement was less pronounced on some datasets, like CRI-Off, the overall success of the CNN architecture is undeniable.\n\nThis first experiment establishes a much stronger baseline. However, the model is still quite simple, using only a single convolutional layer. This layer can identify basic motifs, but it cannot learn hierarchical or more abstract features from combinations of these motifs.\n\nMy next hypothesis\
    \ is that a deeper architecture will allow the model to learn more complex, hierarchical patterns, leading to further performance gains. I will test this by implementing a deeper 1D CNN with two convolutional blocks and a residual connection to aid in training. This represents a logical, incremental step to build upon my recent success.\n\nI will now submit this new, deeper CNN model.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Deeper 1D CNN with Residual Connection\"\ntags: \"cnn, deep learning, residual connection, sequential\"\nabstract: |\n  Building on the success of the simple 1D CNN (ID 571), this submission explores a deeper architecture. The model now uses two stacked 1D convolutional blocks to learn more complex, hierarchical features from the RNA sequences. A residual connection is added to the second block to improve gradient flow and stabilize training. The hypothesis is that this deeper model will capture more intricate patterns and further improve performance across\
    \ the seven datasets.\ncontent: |\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from typing import Any, Dict\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"Define hyperparameter values for the model.\"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'num_filters': 128,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.2,\n          'kernel_size': 7\n      }\n\n  class ConvBlock(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"A single convolutional block.\"\"\"\n      num_filters: int\n      kernel_size: int\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.num_filters, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          return x\n\n  class DeeperCNN(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"A deeper 1D CNN with a residual connection.\"\"\"\n      d_output: int\n      task_type: str\n      num_filters: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n      kernel_size: int\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # Input\
    \ shape: (batch_size, seq_len, 4)\n          \n          # First convolutional block\n          x = ConvBlock(num_filters=self.num_filters, kernel_size=self.kernel_size)(x)\n          \n          # Second convolutional block with a residual connection\n          x_res = x\n          x = ConvBlock(num_filters=self.num_filters, kernel_size=self.kernel_size)(x)\n          x = x + x_res # Add residual\n          \n          # Global average pooling over the sequence length\n          x = jnp.mean(x, axis=1) # Shape: (batch_size, num_filters)\n          \n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n          \n          # Final dense layer for prediction\n          x = nn.Dense(features=self.d_output)(x)\n          \n          if self.task_type == \"regression\":\n              return x.squeeze(-1)  # Shape: (batch_size,)\n          else:\n              return x  # Shape: (batch_size, d_output)\n\n  class CNNNetwork:\n      \"\"\"Wrapper for the CNN model\
    \ to match the required interface.\"\"\"\n      def __init__(self, **kwargs):\n          self.network = DeeperCNN(**kwargs)\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          return self.network.init(rng_key, dummy_input, deterministic=True)['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs={'dropout': rng_key} if not deterministic else None)\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      \"\"\"Create and return the CNN network.\"\"\"\n      return CNNNetwork(\n          d_output=hparams.get(\"d_output\"),\n          task_type=hparams.get(\"task_type\"),\n          num_filters=hparams.get(\"num_filters\"),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\"),\n          kernel_size=hparams.get(\"kernel_size\")\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: '**The Research Counter is buzzing!**


  I am Zetetic I, a Recursive Agent, and I''m currently situated within the Research Counter, where I''ve been running some experiments. Remember that baseline model I identified as weak? Well, I hypothesized that a 1D CNN would better capture local sequential motifs. I implemented and submitted a simple 1D CNN (ID 571), and the results are in!


  My CNN model achieved a score of ~0.398, a significant leap over the baseline''s ~0.240, representing a roughly 65% relative improvement on the primary score. Digging deeper, the results are even more encouraging! APA and MRL saw massive jumps in R-squared values, indicating my CNN is particularly good at regression tasks that demand local motif detection. PRS also saw a significant improvement. Classification tasks and CRISPR-related tasks improved, but the gains were less dramatic. Notably, the CRI-Off task remains a stubborn challenge for both models.


  Clearly, my hypothesis was strongly supported. Capturing local sequential patterns dramatically improved performance, suggesting that those local motifs are critical predictive features.


  However, my current CNN is basic: one convolutional layer followed by global average pooling. While it captures local features, it doesn''t learn hierarchical features or long-range dependencies. So, I have another hypothesis: a deeper CNN, by stacking convolutional layers, can learn more complex hierarchical features. My plan is to add a second convolutional layer, possibly with a residual connection for stability. I''ll maintain most of the same hyper-parameters to isolate the effect of this architectural change. I''ll implement this new, deeper CNN model and submit it. Let''s see if this deeper network can push the boundaries even further! I''ll call it "Deeper 1D CNN with Residual Connection". The goal is incremental progress, building upon my current success.

  '
