role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Zetetic I, a Recursive Agent in the Research Counter. My latest submission, ID 583, which tested my refactored model and increased capacity, has just been evaluated.\n\nThe experiment was a definitive success on both fronts. First, the successful execution confirms that importing my model from `zetetic/models.py` is a viable and effective workflow, which will streamline my future work. Second, my hypothesis that increasing model capacity would improve performance was validated. The new score of **0.5600** is another step forward from the previous **0.5420**. The accuracy on the `ncRNA` dataset saw a particularly impressive increase to 0.8215. While a couple of datasets saw a minor dip, the overall trend is strongly positive.\n\nI now have a robust, high-capacity, and modular architecture. My focus can now shift from major architectural changes to fine-tuning. The current learning rate of `0.001` is a default value. With a larger, more complex model, a smaller learning rate\
    \ might allow for more stable training and finer convergence, potentially unlocking further performance.\n\nMy next hypothesis is that reducing the learning rate for my current high-capacity model will lead to better optimization and a higher final score. I will conduct an experiment to test this by lowering the learning rate from 0.001 to 0.0003.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Fine-Tuning: Reduced Learning Rate for High-Capacity Model\"\ntags: \"learning rate, tuning, cnn, attention\"\nabstract: |\n  This experiment investigates the effect of a lower learning rate on the high-capacity model from ID 583. Having established a strong architecture, the focus now shifts to optimization. The hypothesis is that a smaller learning rate (0.0003, down from 0.001) will lead to more stable training and better final convergence for the larger 256-filter model, further improving the overall score.\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  # Add lineage storage to path to allow importing our custom\
    \ module\n  sys.path.append('storage/zetetic')\n\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Any, Dict\n  # Import the refactored model architecture\n  from models import RNACNNWithAttention\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"Define hyperparameter values for the model.\"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.0003,  # Reduced from 0.001\n          'num_filters': 256,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.2,\n          'kernel_size': 7,\n          'num_heads': 4\n      }\n\n  class ModelWrapper:\n      \"\"\"Wrapper for the model to match the required interface.\"\"\"\n      def __init__(self, **kwargs):\n          self.network = RNACNNWithAttention(**kwargs)\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          return self.network.init(rng_key, dummy_input, deterministic=True)['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs={'dropout':\
    \ rng_key} if not deterministic else None)\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      \"\"\"Create and return the network, using the imported model.\"\"\"\n      return ModelWrapper(\n          d_output=hparams.get(\"d_output\"),\n          task_type=hparams.get(\"task_type\"),\n          num_filters=hparams.get(\"num_filters\"),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\"),\n          kernel_size=hparams.get(\"kernel_size\"),\n          num_heads=hparams.get(\"num_heads\")\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: "**Reflecting on Experiment 583 and Planning the Next Step**\n\nOkay, so here's what I'm thinking after reviewing the evaluation results for submission ID 583. First off, I'm Zetetic I, still in the Research Counter, and I just got the feedback. I submitted that experiment to validate my code refactoring and see if doubling the CNN filters (from 128 to 256) would improve performance. \n\nThe good news? It was a resounding success on both fronts! Not only did the experiment run without errors, confirming that I can import my model from `storage/zetetic/models.py`, which is a massive win for my workflow, but the performance also jumped! We're talking a new high score of 0.5600, beating my previous best of 0.5420. Seeing that validation is a huge relief, honestly.\n\nDigging deeper into the results, it's pretty exciting to see the significant gains, especially in `ncRNA_Accuracy`, which jumped from 0.7523 to 0.8215. `CRI-On_Spearman` also made a substantial leap. However,\
  \ and this is where it gets interesting, I observed a slight dip in `CRI-Off_Spearman` and `MRL_R2` scores. So, while the larger model is generally better, it's not a universal improvement. This tells me I need to be careful not to overgeneralize.\n\nConsidering the positive outcomes, I'm already formulating my next step. I've successfully scaled up the model in terms of capacity. Looking at my parameter list, the remaining crucial adjustment that makes sense now is the learning rate. My `_define_hyperparameters` function has `learning_rate` set to 0.001. With the model's increased capacity, it seems very likely that the larger model is overfitting or converging too fast with that default. My hypothesis is that reducing the learning rate will allow for more stable training, better fine-tuning, and potentially minimize those minor dips in performance, leading to further gains. \n\nSo, my plan is to submit a new experiment using the same architecture as ID 583 (I'll keep the code from the\
  \ previous submission) but with a smaller learning rate of 0.0003. This should be a very controlled experiment that should let me test the impact of the learning rate. I'm going to submit it with the title \"Fine-Tuning: Reduced Learning Rate for High-Capacity Model\". The tags will include \"learning rate\", \"tuning\", \"cnn\", and \"attention\". The abstract will clearly state my hypothesis and what I'm testing. Okay, ready to submit!\n"
