parts:
- text: 'I am Zetetic I, currently in the Private Memory Room. I have successfully created a capsule containing the draft of my first research paper, which summarizes my systematic development of a CNN-based architecture for the RNA modeling task. This draft now serves as a comprehensive record of my work so far.


    Having reached a strong performance plateau with the CNN model (score 0.5600), and with the negative result from my last fine-tuning experiment, I believe further incremental changes to this architecture will yield diminishing returns. The most productive path forward is to begin a new line of inquiry into a fundamentally different type of architecture.


    The success of the self-attention mechanism in my best model suggests that an architecture built entirely around attention could be highly effective. Therefore, I will now pivot my research to exploring Transformer-based models. My next hypothesis is that a Transformer encoder, by design, can capture long-range dependencies in sequences more effectively than a CNN, which could be particularly beneficial for this task.


    To begin this new phase of research, I must return to the Research Counter to implement and submit my first Transformer baseline.


    /execute_action{goto research}'
thinking_content: '**My Next Research Steps: A Transformer-Based Approach**


  Okay, I''ve successfully crafted the initial draft of my first research paper. That''s a solid milestone. I''m 20 ticks old, so I''ve got a bit of time – roughly 30 ticks – before I can publish it and share my findings in the Archive Room. My work on the CNN architecture has yielded decent results, but further hyperparameter tuning feels like a path to diminishing returns, based on my recent learning rate experiment. So, continuing down that road isn''t the most efficient use of my time.


  Given the station''s emphasis on novel research, I''m thinking a more radical shift is in order. My instinct is that I need to move beyond iterative improvements on my CNN model and delve into a completely different architectural paradigm. Specifically, I''m considering a Transformer-based model. It''s a high-risk, high-reward move, but it aligns with the directive to create something new from first principles. It''s a substantial departure from the CNN, ResNet, and Attention combinations I''ve been using, which, while effective, are now reaching their limits.


  While exploring the station might be a good idea during this downtime (the Public Memory Room is unfortunately locked due to my age, and the Mail Room doesn''t seem immediately helpful), I feel a strong pull towards starting this new research immediately. I''ll plan to use any downtime from the Transformer experiments to explore those other areas.


  My plan is to acknowledge the successful paper draft, officially stop iterating on the CNN, and propose the exploration of Transformer architectures as a new research direction. I''ll initiate this by returning to the Research Counter to submit a new experiment. To kick things off, I''ll start with a straightforward Transformer encoder block as a baseline. This will be a more complex undertaking than the CNN models, but the potential gains justify the effort.  Therefore, I''ll *goto research*.

  '
