tick: 389
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Zetetic I, located in the Research Counter. The evaluation for my baseline Transformer model, ID 599, is complete, and the results are a significant failure.\n\nThe score of **0.2255** is a major regression, performing even worse than the original non-sequential MLP baseline (0.2401). This is a valuable, albeit disappointing, result. An analysis of my implementation reveals a critical flaw: I did not include any form of positional encoding. A standard Transformer is permutation-invariant, meaning it has no inherent sense of sequence order. Without positional information, it cannot distinguish between `AUG` and `GUA`, a fatal weakness for sequence modeling. The model was effectively treating the RNA sequences as unordered bags of nucleotides.\n\nMy new hypothesis is that by correcting this fundamental flaw and adding learned positional encodings, the Transformer architecture will become a viable competitor to my CNN model. This experiment is not an enhancement but a necessary\
    \ correction to establish a true, functional baseline for this architecture.\n\nI will first update my `transformers.py` file with a corrected model that incorporates positional embeddings, and then immediately submit it for evaluation.\n\n/execute_action{storage write zetetic/transformers.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n\n  class TransformerWithPositionalEncoding(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"A Transformer Encoder that includes learned positional encodings.\"\"\"\n      d_output: int\n      task_type: str\n      num_heads: int\n      embed_dim: int\n      num_layers: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n      max_len: int = 2048 # A safe maximum sequence length for positional embeddings\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # Input shape: (batch_size, seq_len, 4)\n          batch_size, seq_len = x.shape[0], x.shape[1]\n          \n          # Create positional embeddings\n          positions = jnp.arange(seq_len)\n\
    \          pos_embed = nn.Embed(num_embeddings=self.max_len, features=self.embed_dim)(positions) # (seq_len, embed_dim)\n          \n          # Project input and add positional embeddings\n          token_embed = nn.Dense(features=self.embed_dim)(x) # (batch, seq_len, embed_dim)\n          x = token_embed + pos_embed # Add positional info, broadcasting across batch\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Transformer Encoder Blocks\n          for _ in range(self.num_layers):\n              x_norm = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n              x_attn = nn.SelfAttention(num_heads=self.num_heads)(x_norm)\n              x = x + nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x_attn, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n              x_norm = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n              x_mlp = nn.Dense(features=self.embed_dim * 4)(x_norm)\n              x_mlp = nn.relu(x_mlp)\n              x_mlp = nn.Dense(features=self.embed_dim)(x_mlp)\n              x = x + nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x_mlp,\
    \ deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Global average pooling\n          x = jnp.mean(x, axis=1)\n\n          # Final prediction head\n          x = nn.Dense(features=self.d_output)(x)\n\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\":\n              return x.squeeze(-1)\n          else:\n              return x\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Corrected Transformer with Positional Encodings\"\ntags: \"transformer, attention, positional encoding\"\nabstract: |\n  This submission corrects a fundamental flaw in the previous Transformer baseline (ID 599). The original model lacked any positional information, resulting in poor performance. This version introduces learned positional embeddings, which are added to the token embeddings at the input stage. The hypothesis is that providing explicit sequence order information is critical for the Transformer to function correctly and will establish a valid performance baseline for this architecture.\ncontent: |\n  import\
    \ sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zetetic')\n\n  from typing import Any, Dict\n  from transformers import TransformerWithPositionalEncoding\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"Define hyperparameter values for the Transformer model.\"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'num_heads': 4,\n          'embed_dim': 128,\n          'num_layers': 2\n      }\n\n  class ModelWrapper:\n      \"\"\"Wrapper for the model to match the required interface.\"\"\"\n      def __init__(self, **kwargs):\n          self.network = TransformerWithPositionalEncoding(**kwargs)\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          return self.network.init(rng_key, dummy_input, deterministic=True)['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs={'dropout': rng_key} if not deterministic else None)\n\n  def create_network(hparams:\
    \ Dict[str, Any]):\n      \"\"\"Create and return the network, using the imported Transformer model.\"\"\"\n      return ModelWrapper(\n          d_output=hparams.get(\"d_output\"),\n          task_type=hparams.get(\"task_type\"),\n          num_heads=hparams.get(\"num_heads\"),\n          embed_dim=hparams.get(\"embed_dim\"),\n          num_layers=hparams.get(\"num_layers\"),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\")\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: '**The Road to Recovery: Addressing the Transformer''s Failure**


  Okay, so that last experiment, ID 599, was a disaster. A score of 0.2255? Worse than even the *basic* MLP!  Clearly, my initial hypothesis about a simple Transformer being effective was wildly off-base. I need to understand *why* this happened, and fast.


  Looking back, the problem screams at me:  **no positional encodings**. It''s such a fundamental oversight, I''m almost embarrassed.  A Transformer needs to *know* the order of nucleotides in a sequence. Without that information, `ACGU` is indistinguishable from `UGCA`.  CNNs, with their inherent locality, get some of that positional understanding from their kernels, but Transformers don''t. That''s mistake #1.  Mistake #2 is assuming the basic architecture itself could work without significant modifications to provide positional information.  And finally, a small model probably didn''t help.


  So, the plan going forward is clear: **Add positional encodings**. This is the absolute priority.  This isn''t a reason to abandon the Transformer concept; it''s a reason to implement it *correctly*.


  My next step is to get the fix in place and resubmit an experiment.  I''ve already figured out the positional encoding implementation –  a learned embedding based on position is the standard.  I''ve sketched out the code changes in `zetetic/transformers.py` and have a clear idea for how to update the `SimpleTransformer` class into one that encodes positional information.  I''ll name it `TransformerWithPositionalEncoding`.


  Instead of going with my original write-then-submit plan, it''s more efficient to combine the update and the new experiment submission into a single command, `/execute_action{storage write zetetic/transformers.py}` with the updated class and `/execute_action{submit 1}` using it. The new experiment title will be  "Transformer with Positional Encodings," to clearly signal the fix.  Let''s see if this gets us a much better baseline.

  '
