tick: 396
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Zetetic I, currently in the Research Counter. After encountering difficulties in scaling a pure Transformer model, I have pivoted my strategy. My analysis suggests that combining the local feature extraction power of CNNs with the global relationship modeling of Transformers could yield a superior architecture. To that end, I have just created and saved the code for a new hybrid model in `zetetic/hybrid_models.py`.\n\nMy immediate next step is to submit this hybrid model for evaluation. This experiment will serve as the first test of my new hypothesis. I will use a conservative configuration, combining a single convolutional layer with a small, 2-layer Transformer body, leveraging the parameters from my previous successful models to establish a strong baseline for this new class of architecture.\n\nI will now submit the hybrid model.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Hybrid Architecture: CNN Stem with Transformer Body\"\ntags: \"hybrid, cnn, transformer, attention\"\
    \nabstract: |\n  This submission introduces a novel hybrid architecture that combines a convolutional stem with a Transformer encoder body. The hypothesis is that this model will leverage the strengths of both approaches: the CNN will efficiently extract local sequence motifs, which are then fed into the Transformer to model complex, long-range dependencies between these learned features. This experiment aims to create a model that is more powerful and generalizable than either a pure CNN or a pure Transformer.\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zetetic')\n\n  from typing import Any, Dict\n  from hybrid_models import HybridConvTransformer\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"Define hyperparameters for the Hybrid Conv-Transformer model.\"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          # CNN params\n          'num_filters': 128,\n          'kernel_size': 7,\n          # Transformer params\n          'num_heads':\
    \ 4,\n          'num_layers': 2,\n          # Shared dimension\n          'embed_dim': 128\n      }\n\n  class ModelWrapper:\n      \"\"\"Wrapper for the model to match the required interface.\"\"\"\n      def __init__(self, **kwargs):\n          self.network = HybridConvTransformer(**kwargs)\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          return self.network.init(rng_key, dummy_input, deterministic=True)['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs={'dropout': rng_key} if not deterministic else None)\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      \"\"\"Create and return the network, using the imported hybrid model.\"\"\"\n      return ModelWrapper(\n          d_output=hparams.get(\"d_output\"),\n          task_type=hparams.get(\"task_type\"),\n          num_filters=hparams.get(\"num_filters\"),\n          kernel_size=hparams.get(\"kernel_size\"\
    ),\n          num_heads=hparams.get(\"num_heads\"),\n          num_layers=hparams.get(\"num_layers\"),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\"),\n          embed_dim=hparams.get(\"embed_dim\")\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: '**My Next Experiment: A Hybrid CNN-Transformer**


  Okay, so I''ve finally got the code for this hybrid CNN-Transformer model saved – it''s in `zetetic/hybrid_models.py`. Time to put it to the test. This new architecture is my best shot at breaking through the performance plateau I hit with pure Transformers, so it''s the obvious next step. This experiment will be the ultimate validation of my hybrid model hypothesis!


  First things first, I need to choose some hyperparameters. No need to reinvent the wheel, I''ll leverage what I learned from my past work. For the CNN part, a kernel size of 7 was a sweet spot before, and the number of filters should obviously match the Transformer''s embedding dimension. Speaking of which, a 2-layer Transformer with 128 dimensions worked wonders in my previous runs (ID 603), so I''ll start there. Keeping the layer count low avoids the optimization headaches I faced earlier. Four heads seem to be consistently effective. I''ll settle on an embed dimension of 128. That also means the CNN will have 128 filters.


  As for learning rate, 0.001 is the most reliable starting point I''ve found. For regularization, 0.1 dropout is a solid default.


  Now, let''s assemble the submission. I''ll call it "Hybrid Architecture: CNN Stem with Transformer Body". The tags will be "hybrid, cnn, transformer, attention". For the abstract, I''ll concisely explain the rationale behind this hybrid approach: CNNs for local features, Transformers for global dependencies. I''ll also reiterate my hypothesis that this combined model will outperform both of its pure component architectures.


  The code will be fairly straightforward, importing the HybridConvTransformer class and defining the hyperparameters, and calling the standard wrapper functions. I''ll make sure to get the paths right, using `sys` and `storage/zetetic` as usual.


  Looks like the plan is solid. It''s a logical progression from my previous work, and the code structure reflects my standard, modular approach. Time to get this experiment submitted!

  '
