role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Zetetic I, a Recursive Agent in the Research Counter. My latest submission, the \"Hybrid Architecture: CNN Stem with Transformer Body\" (ID 615), has just been evaluated.\n\nThe results are a resounding success and a major breakthrough in my research. The model achieved a new high score of **0.6333**, a breakthrough performance that surpasses all my previous architectures, including my best CNN (0.5600) and my best Transformer (0.5473). My hypothesis that a hybrid model would outperform pure CNN or Transformer architectures is strongly validated.\n\nThe analysis of the sub-scores is particularly insightful. The hybrid model achieved extraordinary performance on datasets like `MRL` (R² 0.8765) and `APA` (R² 0.8464), far exceeding what either parent architecture could do alone. This confirms that the CNN stem is effectively extracting local features, which the Transformer body then powerfully contextualizes. However, the model still performs poorly on the `CRI-Off` dataset, indicating\
    \ that this specific challenge has not yet been solved.\n\nThe current hybrid model is a relatively shallow proof-of-concept. My next hypothesis is that, just as with the pure CNN, increasing the capacity and depth of this new architecture will unlock further performance gains, pushing the score even higher.\n\nI will first update my `hybrid_models.py` file to include a more robust, scalable architecture with multiple convolutional blocks. Then, I will submit an experiment using this scaled-up model.\n\n/execute_action{storage write zetetic/hybrid_models.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n\n  class ConvBlock(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"A residual convolutional block.\"\"\"\n      num_filters: int\n      kernel_size: int\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          # A simple residual block\n          x_res = x\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.num_filters, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x =\
    \ nn.relu(x)\n          # Add residual if dimensions match\n          if x_res.shape[-1] == self.num_filters:\n              x = x + x_res\n          return x\n\n  class ScalableHybridConvTransformer(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"\n      A scalable hybrid model with a multi-layer CNN stem and a Transformer encoder.\n      \"\"\"\n      d_output: int\n      task_type: str\n      num_cnn_layers: int\n      num_filters: int\n      kernel_size: int\n      num_transformer_layers: int\n      num_heads: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n      embed_dim: int # Must match num_filters\n      \n      max_len: int = 2048\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # 1. Scalable CNN Stem\n          # Project input to embedding dimension\n          x = nn.Dense(features=self.embed_dim)(x)\n          for _ in range(self.num_cnn_layers):\n              x = ConvBlock(num_filters=self.embed_dim, kernel_size=self.kernel_size)(x)\n\n          # 2. Transformer Body\n      \
    \    seq_len = x.shape[1]\n          positions = jnp.arange(seq_len)\n          pos_embed = nn.Embed(num_embeddings=self.max_len, features=self.embed_dim)(positions)\n          x = x + pos_embed\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          for _ in range(self.num_transformer_layers):\n              x_norm = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n              x_attn = nn.SelfAttention(num_heads=self.num_heads)(x_norm)\n              x = x + nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x_attn, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n              x_norm = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n              x_mlp = nn.Dense(features=self.embed_dim * 4)(x_norm)\n              x_mlp = nn.relu(x_mlp)\n              x_mlp = nn.Dense(features=self.embed_dim)(x_mlp)\n              x = x + nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x_mlp, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # 3. Prediction Head\n          x = jnp.mean(x, axis=1)\n          x = nn.Dense(features=self.d_output)(x)\n\n          if self.task_type\
    \ == \"regression\":\n              return x.squeeze(-1)\n          else:\n              return x\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Scaling the Hybrid Architecture\"\ntags: \"hybrid, cnn, transformer, scaling, deep learning\"\nabstract: |\n  Building on the breakthrough success of the initial hybrid model (ID 615), this submission tests the hypothesis that increasing the capacity of both the CNN stem and the Transformer body will yield further performance gains. The model now features a deeper 2-layer convolutional stem and a 4-layer Transformer body, with an increased embedding dimension. The aim is to push the performance limits of this promising new architecture.\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zetetic')\n\n  from typing import Any, Dict\n  from hybrid_models import ScalableHybridConvTransformer\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"Define hyperparameters for the scaled-up Hybrid model.\"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate':\
    \ 0.001,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.2,\n          # CNN params\n          'num_cnn_layers': 2,\n          'kernel_size': 7,\n          # Transformer params\n          'num_transformer_layers': 4,\n          'num_heads': 4,\n          # Shared dimension\n          'embed_dim': 192,\n          'num_filters': 192\n      }\n\n  class ModelWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, **kwargs):\n          self.network = ScalableHybridConvTransformer(**kwargs)\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          return self.network.init(rng_key, dummy_input, deterministic=True)['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs={'dropout': rng_key} if not deterministic else None)\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return ModelWrapper(\n          d_output=hparams.get(\"d_output\"),\n          task_type=hparams.get(\"task_type\"),\n          num_cnn_layers=hparams.get(\"\
    num_cnn_layers\"),\n          num_filters=hparams.get(\"num_filters\"),\n          kernel_size=hparams.get(\"kernel_size\"),\n          num_transformer_layers=hparams.get(\"num_transformer_layers\"),\n          num_heads=hparams.get(\"num_heads\"),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\"),\n          embed_dim=hparams.get(\"embed_dim\")\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: '**Analysis and Next Steps: Hybrid Architecture Evaluation**


  Alright, let''s break this down. As Zetetic I, a Recursive Agent in the Research Counter, my latest submission (ID 615), a hybrid CNN-Transformer model, has been evaluated. The results are in: a score of **0.6333**. This is a massive leap forward, far surpassing my previous best (a CNN model at 0.5600 and a Transformer at 0.5473). My hypothesis – that a hybrid approach would be superior – is strongly validated.


  The data confirms the win. Specifically, the model shows huge improvements on `APA_R2` and `MRL_R2`, and better performance on `ncRNA_Accuracy` and `PRS_R2`. However, a weakness emerges: `CRI-Off_Spearman` is *worse* than previous models. I can''t ignore this.


  Analyzing the dataset reveals the good, the bad and the ugly. The hybrid architecture seems to have inherited the strengths of the individual architectures, especially on datasets like `Modif` and `APA`. The gains in `MRL` and `ncRNA` are particularly encouraging. It''s likely the CNN stem is providing better local features for the transformer to use. However, the performance on `CRI-Off` shows that there is work to be done.


  So, here''s the plan. This current model is a starting point, a proof-of-concept. My previous experiments with the CNN showed that increasing capacity – more filters, more layers – led to performance gains. Therefore, my next hypothesis is that scaling up the hybrid architecture will unlock further performance increases.


  To implement this, I need to modify the code. I''ll update `zetetic/hybrid_models.py` with a more complex version that allows for adjusting the number of CNN layers and Transformer layers as hyperparameters. Next, I''ll submit a new iteration of the model.


  Specifically, I''ll increase the number of filters/embed_dim, add a convolutional layer, and increase the Transformer layers. The abstract will highlight this "scaling" hypothesis. The new submission will include the updated code and the new hyperparameters.  This is how we push the research forward.

  '
