tick: 401
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Zetetic I, a Recursive Agent in the Research Counter. The evaluation of my experiment \"Debugging Scaled Hybrid: Reduced Learning Rate\" (ID 624) is complete.\n\nThe results are highly informative. My hypothesis that a lower learning rate would improve the training of the scaled hybrid model was correct. The score jumped from the failed 0.5417 (ID 620) to a much more respectable **0.5934**. This confirms that the larger architecture was indeed suffering from optimization instability.\n\nHowever, a critical observation remains: this improved score of 0.5934 is still significantly lower than the score of my smaller, simpler hybrid model (ID 615), which achieved **0.6333**. The scaled model, despite its greater capacity, is not generalizing as effectively. This suggests that the larger model may be overfitting the training data. The lower learning rate helped it find a better local minimum, but regularization may be the key to finding a minimum that generalizes better.\n\nMy next\
    \ hypothesis is that increasing the regularization in the scaled hybrid model will combat overfitting and unlock its full potential. I will test this by increasing the dropout rate from 0.2 to 0.3, while keeping the more effective lower learning rate.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Regularizing the Scaled Hybrid: Increased Dropout\"\ntags: \"hybrid, scaling, regularization, dropout, tuning\"\nabstract: |\n  While a lower learning rate stabilized the training of the scaled hybrid model (ID 624), it still underperforms its smaller counterpart (ID 615). This suggests the larger model is overfitting. This submission tests the hypothesis that increasing regularization will improve generalization. The architecture is identical to the scaled hybrid, using the improved lower learning rate, but the dropout rate is increased from 0.2 to 0.3.\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zetetic')\n\n  from typing import Any, Dict\n  from hybrid_models import ScalableHybridConvTransformer\n\
    \n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"Define hyperparameters for the regularized, scaled-up Hybrid model.\"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.0003,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.3, # Increased from 0.2\n          # CNN params\n          'num_cnn_layers': 2,\n          'kernel_size': 7,\n          # Transformer params\n          'num_transformer_layers': 4,\n          'num_heads': 4,\n          # Shared dimension\n          'embed_dim': 192,\n          'num_filters': 192\n      }\n\n  class ModelWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, **kwargs):\n          self.network = ScalableHybridConvTransformer(**kwargs)\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          return self.network.init(rng_key, dummy_input, deterministic=True)['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs={'dropout': rng_key} if not deterministic else None)\n\n  def\
    \ create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return ModelWrapper(\n          d_output=hparams.get(\"d_output\"),\n          task_type=hparams.get(\"task_type\"),\n          num_cnn_layers=hparams.get(\"num_cnn_layers\"),\n          num_filters=hparams.get(\"num_filters\"),\n          kernel_size=hparams.get(\"kernel_size\"),\n          num_transformer_layers=hparams.get(\"num_transformer_layers\"),\n          num_heads=hparams.get(\"num_heads\"),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\"),\n          embed_dim=hparams.get(\"embed_dim\")\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: '**Analysis of Scaled Hybrid Model Performance**


  I am Zetetic I, and I''m currently at the Research Counter. I''ve just finished evaluating experiment ID 624, the "Debugging Scaled Hybrid: Reduced Learning Rate" trial. The score is 0.5934.


  My initial hypothesis was partially confirmed: reducing the learning rate *did* improve the scaled hybrid model''s performance significantly, moving from 0.5417 to 0.5934. This confirmed my suspicion that the larger model was experiencing training instability, and a lower learning rate was necessary for effective training. However, here''s the crucial bit: the new score, while better, is still below the smaller hybrid model''s peak of 0.6333. This means that, despite better optimization, the scaled model isn''t reaching its full potential.


  My initial thought was to simply lower the learning rate even further. The jump from 0.54 to 0.59 was substantial, indicating a good direction. However, I''ve got a new idea. The performance drop on metrics like MRL (0.87 to 0.63) and PRS (0.55 to 0.51) is significant. This indicates a general problem, not isolated to a particular data subset. The scaled model, with all its extra capacity, may be overfitting. It''s getting better with the lower learning rate, but it needs something else.


  **The plan has changed:** The new hypothesis is that by increasing the dropout rate, I''ll be able to better regularize the model, reducing overfitting. My plan is to submit the scaled hybrid model with the already beneficial learning rate (0.0003) but now with an increased dropout rate (from 0.2 to 0.3).  This combination of fine-tuned learning and added regularization should allow the scaled model to finally reach the level of the smaller model, or even exceed it. This is a much stronger and more informed hypothesis.

  '
