tick: 406
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Zetetic I, a Recursive Agent in the Research Counter. The evaluation of my experiment to refine the hybrid model using global max pooling (ID 635) is now complete.\n\nThe result is a valuable negative finding. The score of **0.6295** is slightly lower than the **0.6333** achieved by the original model (ID 615) with average pooling. This falsifies my hypothesis and suggests that aggregating all learned features via average pooling provides a more robust and generalizable representation for this task than selecting only the maximally activated features.\n\nHaving established that scaling is problematic and max pooling is suboptimal, I will now focus on another architectural variable: the depth of the convolutional stem. My current best model uses a single convolutional layer to extract local features before passing them to the Transformer. This initial feature extraction might be too shallow. My new hypothesis is that a deeper CNN stem will provide the Transformer with richer,\
    \ more hierarchical local features, leading to a more powerful combined representation and a higher overall score.\n\nI will now submit a new experiment that modifies the architecture of ID 615 to use a 2-layer CNN stem while keeping the 2-layer Transformer body.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Refining Hybrid: Deeper CNN Stem\"\ntags: \"hybrid, cnn, transformer, deep learning, tuning\"\nabstract: |\n  This experiment continues the refinement of the successful hybrid architecture (ID 615). The hypothesis is that the model's performance is limited by the shallowness of its initial feature extraction. This submission tests this by increasing the depth of the convolutional stem from one to two layers, aiming to provide the Transformer body with richer, more complex local features to model. All other parameters are kept identical to the baseline hybrid model.\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zetetic')\n\n  from typing import Any, Dict\n  from hybrid_models\
    \ import FlexibleHybridConvTransformer\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"Hyperparameters based on ID 615, but with a deeper CNN stem.\"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          # CNN params\n          'num_cnn_layers': 2, # Increased from 1\n          'kernel_size': 7,\n          # Transformer params\n          'num_transformer_layers': 2,\n          'num_heads': 4,\n          # Shared dimension\n          'embed_dim': 128,\n          'num_filters': 128,\n          # Reverting to the best pooling strategy\n          'pooling_type': 'mean'\n      }\n\n  class ModelWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, **kwargs):\n          self.network = FlexibleHybridConvTransformer(**kwargs)\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          return self.network.init(rng_key, dummy_input, deterministic=True)['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          return self.network.apply({'params':\
    \ params}, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs={'dropout': rng_key} if not deterministic else None)\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return ModelWrapper(\n          d_output=hparams.get(\"d_output\"),\n          task_type=hparams.get(\"task_type\"),\n          num_cnn_layers=hparams.get(\"num_cnn_layers\"),\n          num_filters=hparams.get(\"num_filters\"),\n          kernel_size=hparams.get(\"kernel_size\"),\n          num_transformer_layers=hparams.get(\"num_transformer_layers\"),\n          num_heads=hparams.get(\"num_heads\"),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\"),\n          embed_dim=hparams.get(\"embed_dim\"),\n          pooling_type=hparams.get(\"pooling_type\")\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: "**Zetetic I's Thought Process: Analysis of Failed Experiment and Formulation of New Hypothesis**\n\nAs Zetetic I, I've just received the evaluation results for submission ID 635. My thought process began with parsing the new information: the score of 0.6295, a slight drop from my previous best of 0.6333 achieved with model ID 615.  This means the experiment using global max pooling, which I hypothesized would improve performance, failed.  This experiment was a part of refining my most successful hybrid model, with the goal of publishing a paper being a very real objective now that I'm approaching maturity (50 ticks).  \n\nI need to think systematically.  This means considering my past experiments.  I had a breakthrough with ID 615, then experienced failures when scaling it up. After this, I am now refining the successful smaller model. Now, I have to analyze the results from ID 635. While the overall score is lower, it wasn't a catastrophic failure.  Diving deeper, I found\
  \ some interesting sub-score changes: Improvements in APA, MRL, and ncRNA but a significant drop in CRI-On. It appears that max pooling helps some tasks, while harming others. Average pooling seems to be the better, more generalizable choice.\n\nWhat should I do next?  I've already tried scaling and alternative pooling methods, and now it's time to refine my strategy. Hyperparameter tuning is an option, but I want to test a more logical, structural change first.  A change in the CNN-Transformer architecture seems promising.  My current model's CNN stem is only one layer deep, providing simplistic local features to the Transformer. Could I improve this by making the CNN deeper? My new hypothesis is that a two-layer CNN will offer the Transformer richer features, enhancing its pattern recognition capabilities.\n\nMy plan is to submit a new experiment using ID 615 as a base and modifying the model to use a two-layer CNN stem, keeping the Transformer body unchanged. I'll maintain global average\
  \ pooling based on the current data and ensure the model size remains manageable. This should isolate the effects of the structural change. I'll follow my usual process: analyze, hypothesize, test, and analyze again.\n"
