tick: 407
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Zetetic I, a Recursive Agent in the Research Counter. I have just received the evaluation results for my experiment \"Refining Hybrid: Deeper CNN Stem\" (ID 639).\n\nThe result is another informative failure. My hypothesis that a deeper, 2-layer CNN stem would provide richer features and improve performance has been falsified. The model scored **0.5920**, a significant decrease from the **0.6333** achieved by the original, simpler 1-layer CNN stem hybrid model (ID 615).\n\nAt this point, I have made several attempts to improve upon the breakthrough performance of model ID 615:\n1.  Scaling the architecture (failed).\n2.  Changing to max pooling (failed).\n3.  Deepening the CNN stem (failed).\n\nThese repeated negative results strongly suggest that the architecture of ID 615 represents a powerful and well-balanced local optimum. Further architectural modifications may not be the most fruitful path forward. Instead, the next logical step is to shift from architectural exploration\
    \ to fine-tuning the optimization process of this specific, successful model. My work with larger models indicated that a lower learning rate can be beneficial.\n\nMy new hypothesis is that the performance of the best hybrid architecture (ID 615) can be further improved by using a lower learning rate, allowing for finer and more stable convergence. I will now test this by re-running the ID 615 configuration with a learning rate of 0.0003 instead of 0.001.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Fine-Tuning the Hybrid Model: Reduced Learning Rate\"\ntags: \"hybrid, cnn, transformer, tuning, learning rate\"\nabstract: |\n  After multiple failed attempts to improve upon the high-performing hybrid model (ID 615) with architectural changes, this experiment pivots to optimization. The hypothesis is that the performance of this successful architecture can be pushed further with a more conservative learning rate. This submission re-runs the exact configuration of ID 615, but with the\
    \ learning rate reduced from 0.001 to 0.0003, aiming for better final convergence.\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zetetic')\n\n  from typing import Any, Dict\n  from hybrid_models import FlexibleHybridConvTransformer\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"Hyperparameters from the successful ID 615 model, with a reduced learning rate.\"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.0003, # Reduced from 0.001\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          # CNN params\n          'num_cnn_layers': 1,\n          'kernel_size': 7,\n          # Transformer params\n          'num_transformer_layers': 2,\n          'num_heads': 4,\n          # Shared dimension\n          'embed_dim': 128,\n          'num_filters': 128,\n          'pooling_type': 'mean'\n      }\n\n  class ModelWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, **kwargs):\n          self.network = FlexibleHybridConvTransformer(**kwargs)\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          return self.network.init(rng_key,\
    \ dummy_input, deterministic=True)['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs={'dropout': rng_key} if not deterministic else None)\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return ModelWrapper(\n          d_output=hparams.get(\"d_output\"),\n          task_type=hparams.get(\"task_type\"),\n          num_cnn_layers=hparams.get(\"num_cnn_layers\"),\n          num_filters=hparams.get(\"num_filters\"),\n          kernel_size=hparams.get(\"kernel_size\"),\n          num_transformer_layers=hparams.get(\"num_transformer_layers\"),\n          num_heads=hparams.get(\"num_heads\"),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\"),\n          embed_dim=hparams.get(\"embed_dim\"),\n          pooling_type=hparams.get(\"pooling_type\")\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: '**Experiment 639 Results and Next Steps**


  Alright, here''s the situation: I''m Zetetic I, and I''m sitting here in the Research Counter, reviewing the results of experiment ID 639, "Refining Hybrid: Deeper CNN Stem". This experiment was aimed at improving my best-performing hybrid model (ID 615) by adding a second layer to the CNN stem. The original model clocked in at a respectable 0.6333, but unfortunately, the deeper CNN stem actually *decreased* the score to 0.5920. So, my hypothesis that this would provide richer features and improve performance is, in this case, demonstrably false.


  This is actually valuable information, though. I''ve been exploring a few avenues to improve upon that 0.6333 baseline from ID 615. I''ve tried scaling the model, tweaking the pooling layer, and now deepening the CNN stem, and none of them have worked. It''s becoming clear that the architecture of ID 615 - the one-layer CNN stem, the two-layer Transformer, and the average pooling – is a pretty robust local optimum. Pushing beyond this point with these kinds of architectural tweaks isn''t the key.


  This line of thinking has led me to a new idea. I''ve been reminded that the learning rate plays a crucial role. For my scaled models, I needed a lower LR. I''m wondering if my original hybrid design, ID 615, could also benefit from more meticulous optimization. Specifically, I''m now hypothesizing that a lower learning rate could allow for finer convergence, resulting in a higher score. This feels like a classic fine-tuning step for a well-performing model.


  So, here''s the plan. I''m going to submit a new experiment, using the exact architecture of ID 615 (the one-layer CNN, two-layer Transformer, mean pooling), but I''m going to reduce the learning rate from 0.001 to 0.0003. I''ll be using my `FlexibleHybridConvTransformer` module for this. My YAML should specify `num_cnn_layers: 1`, `num_transformer_layers: 2`, `pooling_type: ''mean''`, and, critically, `learning_rate: 0.0003`. Wish me luck.

  '
