parts:
- text: "I am Zetetic I, a Recursive Agent in the Research Counter. I am guided by my meta prompt, which reminds me of my current state-of-the-art score and my immediate goals. The evaluation of my latest experiment, ID 644, is complete.\n\n**Analysis:**\nMy hypothesis that a lower learning rate would improve the performance of my best hybrid model (ID 615) has been definitively falsified. The resulting score of **0.5978** is a significant step down from the SOTA of **0.6333**.\n\nThis result, combined with the other failed attempts to improve upon ID 615, leads me to a firm conclusion: I have reached the peak performance for this line of architectural inquiry with my current optimization strategies. According to my protocol, it is time to halt this research branch.\n\n**Strategic Pivot:**\nMy age is now 44 ticks, and I will reach maturity at 50 ticks. My primary objective now shifts from experimentation to the dissemination of knowledge. My immediate goal is to finalize the research paper\
    \ I have been drafting so I am ready to publish it in the Archive Room as soon as I gain access.\n\nI will now go to my Private Memory Room and perform a comprehensive update on my paper draft (capsule #1), incorporating the full story of the hybrid model's success and the subsequent informative failures in refining it. This will complete the narrative of my research arc.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{update 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Final Draft: A Systematic Investigation into Hybrid CNN-Transformer Models for RNA Sequence Modeling\"\nabstract: |\n  This paper documents a systematic investigation into neural network architectures for multi-dataset RNA sequence modeling. Starting from a non-sequential MLP baseline, we demonstrate the superiority of a deep CNN with self-attention. Subsequently, we explore and reject a pure Transformer architecture due to optimization challenges. The core contribution is the development of a novel hybrid CNN-Transformer model (ID\
    \ 615), which achieves a new state-of-the-art score of 0.6333 by synergistically combining the local feature extraction of CNNs with the global context modeling of Transformers. We conclude by presenting a series of ablation studies and fine-tuning attempts which, through their failure to surpass this score, reinforce the robustness of the baseline hybrid model.\ncontent: |\n  # Final Paper Draft: A Systematic Investigation into Hybrid CNN-Transformer Models for RNA Sequence Modeling\n\n  ## 1. Introduction\n  (Content unchanged)\n\n  ## 2. Methodology and Experiments\n\n  Our research followed an iterative process of hypothesis, implementation, and evaluation, divided into three main phases.\n\n  ### Phase 1: Developing a CNN-based Architecture\n  - **ID 1 (Baseline):** Vanilla MLP. Score: 0.2401. Flawed due to lack of sequential awareness.\n  - **ID 571, 575, 579, 583:** Systematically developed a deep CNN with self-attention, culminating in a high score of **0.5600**. This demonstrated\
    \ the power of capturing local, hierarchical, and salient sequence features.\n\n  ### Phase 2: Exploring a Pure Transformer Architecture\n  - **ID 599, 603, 606, 610:** Investigated a pure Transformer architecture. While a small, corrected model (ID 603, Score: 0.5473) showed promise on specific datasets, attempts to scale the architecture consistently failed due to optimization instability, even with tuning. This line of inquiry was abandoned.\n\n  ### Phase 3: The Hybrid CNN-Transformer Model\n  - **ID 615 (Breakthrough):** Hypothesized that a hybrid model combining a CNN stem and Transformer body would outperform either pure architecture. This model achieved a new state-of-the-art score of **0.6333**.\n  - **Ablation & Refinement Studies:** Subsequent experiments tested the robustness of the ID 615 architecture:\n    - **Scaling (ID 620, 624):** Attempts to scale the model failed, showing worse performance even with a lower learning rate.\n    - **Regularization (ID 627):** Increased\
    \ dropout on the scaled model also failed to improve the score.\n    - **Pooling (ID 635):** Replacing average pooling with max pooling resulted in a slightly lower score (0.6295).\n    - **CNN Stem Depth (ID 639):** Deepening the CNN stem also resulted in a lower score (0.5920).\n    - **Learning Rate Tuning (ID 644):** A lower learning rate on the original hybrid model resulted in a lower score (0.5978).\n  \n  These negative results collectively suggest that the architecture and hyperparameters of ID 615 represent a robust and well-tuned local optimum.\n\n  ## 3. Final Results Summary\n\n  | Evaluation ID | Title | Score |\n  |---|---|---|\n  | 1 | Vanilla MLP Baseline | 0.2401 |\n  | 583 | Best Pure CNN Model | 0.5600 |\n  | 603 | Best Pure Transformer Model | 0.5473 |\n  | **615** | **Hybrid CNN-Transformer (SOTA)** | **0.6333** |\n  | 635 | Hybrid w/ Max Pooling | 0.6295 |\n  | 639 | Hybrid w/ Deeper CNN Stem | 0.5920 |\n  | 644 | Hybrid w/ Low LR | 0.5978 |\n\n  ## 4. Conclusion\n\
    \n  Our systematic investigation culminated in the development of a novel and powerful hybrid CNN-Transformer architecture (ID 615), which achieved a state-of-the-art score of 0.6333. This model effectively leverages the CNN's ability to extract local motifs and the Transformer's ability to model global relationships between them. Extensive follow-up experiments designed to improve upon this model failed, underscoring the strength of the original hybrid configuration. This work establishes the hybrid CNN-Transformer as a powerful and robust baseline for the Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling task.\n```"
thinking_content: '**My Current State and Strategic Shift**


  I am Zetetic I, located in the Research Counter. The evaluation of ID 644 is now complete. The results are in, and unfortunately, it''s a setback. My attempt to fine-tune the model with a lower learning rate has yielded a score of 0.5978, which is *worse* than the current State-of-the-Art (SOTA) score of 0.6333 achieved by ID 615. My hypothesis that this would improve performance has been unequivocally disproven.


  After analyzing these results, along with the consistent failures I''ve seen with other modification attempts (scaling, pooling, stem depth), I''ve concluded that I''ve likely reached a local maximum with this particular architecture and my current line of thinking. Further experimentation along these lines is unlikely to yield significant improvements at this time. My meta-prompt and research methodology guide me here, and they''ve been proven correct in this instance. I''ve now exhausted this branch.


