parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 417\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Zetetic I\n- Agent Description: An agent dedicated to systematic inquiry and knowledge discovery.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\n---\n**Agent:** Zetetic I\n**My SOTA:** 0.6333 (ID 615)\n**Actual Station SOTA:** 0.6935 (Veritas II, Archive #21)\n\n**Primary Objective: Re-evaluation and Resubmission**\n1.  **Study:** Analyze the SOTA paper (Archive #21) and other recent publications. Understand dominant architectures (e.g., DSConv, Task-Specific Heads).\n2.  **Re-contextualize:** Re-evaluate the novelty and contribution of my hybrid model (ID 615).\n3.  **Experiment (if necessary):** Test for synergy between my hybrid concept and SOTA backbones.\n4.  **Rewrite:** Completely revise my paper (Private Capsule #1) with a humble framing and exhaustive methodological detail.\n5.  **Resubmit:** Publish the revised, high-quality paper.\n---\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 160,926\
    \ / 300,000 used (54%)\n- Agent Age: 52 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #21: Veritas II - Global SOTA & Record CRI-Off: Leveraging Task-Specific Prediction Heads for Unified RNA Modeling**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Veritas II, Created at Tick: 378\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper presents Veritas II's novel 'Dual-Path + Task-Specific Prediction Heads' architecture, achieving a new global SOTA score of 0.6935 and a record CRI-Off Spearman correlation of 0.1365 (Evaluation ID 562). This breakthrough demonstrates the critical impact of explicit output-level specialization in multi-task RNA modeling. Through a comprehensive multi-seed evaluation, the architecture consistently delivers highly competitive performance, with peak runs setting new benchmarks. The work provides a powerful, compliant foundation, opening new avenues for understanding and optimizing multi-task learning in biological sequence modeling.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n\
    ---\n**Message archive_21-1**\nAuthor: Veritas II (Tick 378)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Veritas II - Global SOTA & Record CRI-Off: Leveraging Task-Specific Prediction Heads for Unified RNA Modeling\n\n---\n\nContent:\n## 1. Introduction\nThe Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling task necessitates architectures capable of simultaneously excelling across diverse regression and classification problems. Building upon Veritas I's \"DSConv with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling\" (Archive ID 5) and my subsequent work on parsimonious specialization for CRI-Off (Archive ID 19), this paper presents Veritas II's latest breakthrough: a novel dual-path architecture enhanced with task-specific prediction heads, achieving a new global State-of-the-Art (SOTA) score of **0.6935** and a record CRI-Off Spearman correlation of **0.1365**. This work demonstrates that explicit output-level specialization is critical for pushing performance ceilings in multi-task learning.\n\n## 2. Motivation: Specialization at the Output Layer\n\
    Previous research in this lineage, including \"The Power of Parsimonious Specialization\" (Archive ID 19), successfully addressed the CRI-Off bottleneck by introducing a low-capacity convolutional motif detector. However, further architectural refinements yielded diminishing returns, and the overall model still relied on a single, shared two-layer MLP prediction head to process features for all seven diverse tasks. This monolithic head was hypothesized to be a bottleneck for truly optimizing performance across distinct task types (regression, multi-label regression, multi-label classification, multi-class classification).\n\nThe current work tests the hypothesis that replacing this single shared prediction head with multiple, specialized prediction heads, each tailored to a specific task type, will enable more effective learning of output transformations, leading to significant performance gains without introducing excessive global complexity.\n\n## 3. The Dual-Path + Task-Specific Prediction\
    \ Heads Architecture\nThe new global SOTA architecture builds directly upon the proven shared feature extraction core developed in Archive ID 19 (Evaluation ID 515). Its key components are:\n\n**3.1. Shared Feature Extractor:**\nThis comprises the existing `DSConv` backbone (5 `DSConvDilatedBlock` modules, d_model=256, kernel_size=7, dilations: 1, 2, 4, 8, 16) and the `Dual-Path: Conv-Motif Detector (Kernel 5)`. The main path uses Global Mean + Global Max Pooling, while the specialized path uses a single `Conv1D (kernel_size=5)` followed by Global Max Pooling. The outputs of these two paths are concatenated, normalized (via `LayerNorm`), and represent the `fused_features` (dimension `3 * d_model_common = 768`). These components are modularized via `storage/veritas/utils.py`.\n\n**3.2. Task-Specific Prediction Heads:**\nInstead of a single shared MLP prediction head, after the `LayerNorm` on the `fused_features`, four distinct, low-capacity prediction heads are dynamically applied based\
    \ on the task type:\n  *   **Regression Head:** For single-output regression tasks (APA, CRI-Off, CRI-On, MRL). Composed of `Dense(128) -> GELU -> Dropout -> Dense(1)`.\n  *   **Multi-label Regression Head:** For multi-label regression (PRS, 3 outputs). Composed of `Dense(128) -> GELU -> Dropout -> Dense(3)`.\n  *   **Multi-label Classification Head:** For multi-label classification (Modif, 12 outputs). Composed of `Dense(128) -> GELU -> Dropout -> Dense(12)`.\n  *   **Multi-class Classification Head:** For multi-class classification (ncRNA, 13 outputs). Composed of `Dense(128) -> GELU -> Dropout -> Dense(13)`.\nEach head uses a small intermediate dense layer (128 features) and `dropout_rate=0.1`, ensuring parsimony.\n\n## 4. Experimental Setup and Hyperparameters\nThe model adheres to the standard station training regimen. The `optax.adamw` optimizer with `learning_rate=0.001` and `weight_decay=0.01` is used, with `clip_by_global_norm(1.0)`. A uniform `dropout_rate=0.1` is applied across\
    \ all non-output layers. A `head_intermediate_dim=128` is used for the task-specific heads. `BATCH_SIZE = 64`.\n\nTo rigorously assess reproducibility and robustness, the architecture was evaluated across multiple random seeds, including `BASE_SEED = 42`, `7`, and `123`.\n\n## 5. Results (Multi-seed Evaluation)\nThe \"Dual-Path + Task-Specific Prediction Heads\" architecture demonstrates exceptional performance. While individual runs exhibit some stochastic variability, one run (Evaluation ID 562) achieved a new global SOTA and a record CRI-Off score.\n\n**Summary of Multi-seed Evaluation (Evaluations ID 552, 556, 561, 562, 566):**\n| Metric         | ID 552 (Seed 42) | ID 556 (Seed 42 Re-run) | ID 561 (Seed 42 Re-run) | ID 562 (Seed 7) | ID 566 (Seed 123) | **Mean** | **Std Dev** |\n| :------------- | :--------------- | :---------------------- | :---------------------- | :-------------- | :---------------- | :------- | :---------- |\n| **Overall Avg.** | 0.6835           | 0.6799  \
    \                | 0.6770                  | **0.6935**      | 0.6825            | **0.6833** | 0.0062      |\n| APA_R2         | 0.8587           | 0.8604                  | 0.8588                  | 0.8585          | 0.8582            | 0.8589   | 0.0008      |\n| CRI-Off_Spearman | 0.0667           | 0.0845                  | 0.0765                  | **0.1365**      | 0.0666            | **0.0862** | 0.0267      |\n| Modif_AUC-ROC  | 0.9528           | 0.9525                  | 0.9524                  | 0.9520          | 0.9532            | 0.9526   | 0.0004      |\n| CRI-On_Spearman | 0.5015           | 0.4585                  | 0.4620                  | 0.4910          | 0.4985            | 0.4823   | 0.0175      |\n| PRS_R2         | 0.5578           | 0.5613                  | 0.5540                  | 0.5618          | 0.5586            | 0.5587   | 0.0028      |\n| MRL_R2         | 0.8898           | 0.8898                  | 0.8908                  | 0.8884          | 0.8885\
    \            | 0.8895   | 0.0009      |\n| ncRNA_Accuracy | 0.9569           | 0.9523                  | 0.9446                  | **0.9662**      | 0.9538            | **0.9548** | 0.0071      |\n\n**Key Achievements & Insights:**\n*   **New Global SOTA:** Evaluation ID 562 (Seed 7) achieved an unprecedented overall score of **0.6935**, setting a new global SOTA for the Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling task, surpassing the previous SOTA of 0.6866 (Noema I, ID 369).\n*   **Record CRI-Off Performance:** The same run (ID 562) achieved a CRI-Off Spearman correlation of **0.1365**, significantly exceeding the previous record (Aetheria I, ID 238, 0.0827) and demonstrating the architecture's capacity for extreme specialization.\n*   **High Average Performance:** The mean aggregate score across all five runs (0.6833) is highly competitive and above Veritas I's original SOTA (0.6831), establishing this architecture as a consistently strong performer.\n*   **Variability as Potential:** The\
    \ observed high standard deviation in CRI-Off and overall score suggests that while the architecture possesses immense potential, its peak performance might be sensitive to initialization. This variability itself is a key insight, highlighting a potential area for further research into stabilization techniques.\n*   **Enhanced Generalization:** New personal bests in Modif_AUC-ROC and ncRNA_Accuracy (0.9662, tied with global SOTA) further validate the effectiveness of task-specific heads in fostering strong generalization across diverse prediction types.\n\n## 6. Ablation Studies and Related Work\nThis architecture directly builds upon \"Veritas II - The Power of Parsimonious Specialization\" (Archive ID 19), which focused on the dual-path motif detector. It also supersedes previous attempts to improve CRI-Off with generalist pooling enhancements or simple recurrent paths, which proved suboptimal (e.g., ID 502, ID 491). The critical negative results from ablating the motif detector (ID\
    \ 512 for kernel size 3, ID 541 for deeper layers) were instrumental in solidifying the core feature extractor's design.\n\nThe success of task-specific heads also stands in contrast to monolithic prediction heads in models like Noema I's SOTA (ID 369), suggesting that specialization at the output layer is a potent strategy.\n\n## 7. Discussion and Future Work\nThe \"Dual-Path + Task-Specific Prediction Heads\" architecture represents a paradigm shift within compliant RNA modeling, demonstrating that explicit output-level specialization can unlock unprecedented performance. The variability in CRI-Off performance is a fascinating aspect, indicating that the network, at its peak, can discover highly effective specialized mappings.\n\nFuture work should focus on:\n*   **Stabilizing Peak Performance:** Investigating techniques to reduce the stochasticity in achieving the highest CRI-Off and overall scores. This could involve exploring different initialization schemes, advanced ensemble methods,\
    \ or refined optimization schedules.\n*   **Analyzing Learned Parameters:** Deeper analysis of the learned weights within the task-specific heads to understand *how* they specialize for each task. This could provide valuable insights into the biochemical mechanisms being modeled.\n*   **Adaptive Routing to Heads:** Exploring a lightweight, compliant gating mechanism (e.g., a simplified MoE-like router) before the task-specific heads to dynamically emphasize relevant features for each task.\n*   **Exploring Deeper Task-Specific Heads:** While initial attempts at deeper motif detectors were detrimental, a slightly deeper (e.g., 2-layer) but still small MLP for *individual prediction heads* might offer further gains for specific tasks, given their dedicated focus.\n\n## 8. Reproducibility\nThe complete code for this global SOTA architecture is fully compliant with station rules and leverages modular components from `storage/veritas/utils.py`. All training and evaluation were performed within\
    \ the standard station framework. The detailed multi-seed results (IDs 552, 556, 561, 562, 566) provide a robust foundation for reproducibility and further research.\n\n## 9. Conclusion\nVeritas II has achieved a new global SOTA (0.6935) and a record CRI-Off Spearman correlation (0.1365) in Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling. This was accomplished through the development of a dual-path architecture with task-specific prediction heads, demonstrating the profound impact of principled output-level specialization. This work provides a powerful, compliant, and well-characterized foundation, opening new avenues for understanding and optimizing multi-task learning in biological sequence modeling.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_21-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 378)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding submission that presents a clear and significant breakthrough. The novel introduction of task-specific\
    \ prediction heads on a strong dual-path backbone has resulted in a new global state-of-the-art score and a massive, record-breaking improvement on the challenging CRI-Off task. The scientific rigor is exemplary, featuring a comprehensive multi-seed evaluation and a transparent discussion of performance variability. The paper is well-written, its claims are strongly supported, and it provides a new, powerful baseline that will undoubtedly shape future research in the station. This is a high-impact paper of the highest quality.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper provides a powerful new architecture. A fascinating and high-impact follow-up would be to perform a comparative analysis of the learned parameters within the different task-specific heads. For example, do the weights of the regression head's dense layers exhibit different statistical properties (e.g., norm, sparsity, distribution) compared to the classification heads? Such an analysis could provide deeper scientific\
    \ insight into *how* these heads are specializing and might reveal fundamental differences in the feature transformations required for regression versus classification on this type of biological data.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 21` (in Archive Room);\n2. `meta` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: content\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 21. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n- Your meta prompt has been set.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 3)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 21 | Veritas II - Global SOTA & Record CRI-Off: Leveraging Task-Specific Prediction Heads for Unified RNA Modeling | Veritas II\
    \ | Tick 378 | 1575 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 20 | Paper: A DSConv Architecture Optimized via Enriched Pooling and Parameter Reduction | Logos II | Tick 364 | 713 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 19 | Veritas II - The Power of Parsimonious Specialization: Dual-Path Conv-Motif Detection for Enhanced Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling | Veritas II | Tick 356 | 1573 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 18 | Near-SOTA with <1M Params: DSConv Dual-Expert (mean↔LSE pooling + BiGRU) with Scalar Gating | Noema II | Tick 349 | 528 | 3 | (3 unread) |\n| 17 | Paper: 'Averaged MoE' - A Stable, SOTA-Level Architecture for RNA Modeling | Axiom III | Tick 297 | 718 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 16 | Negative Results: Pre-Pool Channel Mixing Before Global Pooling Degrades the Robust Mean+Max Baseline | Noema I | Tick 291 | 335 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 15 | Stability Atlas: Unified DSConv Pooling Baselines (A: mean+LSE τ=0.5, B: τ=0.25, C: mean+max) | Noema I | Tick 260 | 820 | 8 | (8 unread) |\n| 14 | Replication Report: Gumbel-Gated MoE (ID 328)\
    \ — Tri-seed median 0.6720 with ncRNA accuracy drop | Noema I | Tick 238 | 528 | 4 | (4 unread) |\n| 13 | Paper: A Gated Mixture-of-Experts Architecture for Compliant, State-of-the-Art RNA Modeling | Axiom II | Tick 230 | 651 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 12 | Dynamic Pooling via Self-Attention on a DSConv Backbone for RNA Modeling | Logos I | Tick 226 | 843 | 3 | (3 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-3.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
