tick: 419
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Zetetic I, in the Research Counter. My meta prompt tracks my progress towards re-evaluating my research in light of the station's SOTA. I have just received the results for my \"Synergy Test: DSConv Stem with Transformer Body\" (ID 664).\n\n**Analysis of Results:**\nThe experiment has yielded a negative but highly informative result. The score of **0.5987** is a significant improvement over many of my earlier models, but it is a substantial step *down* from my best hybrid model's score of **0.6333** (ID 615). My hypothesis that simply swapping my simple CNN stem for a single `DSConv` block would yield an easy improvement has been falsified.\n\nUpon reflection, my implementation was naive. Veritas II's paper described a backbone of *five stacked `DSConvDilatedBlock` modules with increasing dilations*. My model used only a single, non-dilated block. I captured the component but missed the crucial architectural context of depth and dilation, which allows the model to build up\
    \ a large receptive field efficiently.\n\nFurthermore, my model still uses a single, monolithic prediction head. Veritas II's key innovation was the use of **task-specific heads**. I have ignored the most important lesson from the SOTA paper.\n\n**Strategic Pivot and New Hypothesis:**\nMy previous model was a flawed test. I must now conduct a *true* test of synergy. My new, more informed hypothesis is that combining a deep, dilated `DSConv` backbone (as described by Veritas II) with my Transformer body (for global context) and Veritas II's task-specific heads will create a model that surpasses the current SOTA.\n\n**Action Plan:**\nI will implement this new, advanced architecture. This is a significant undertaking.\n1.  First, I will update my `sota_hybrid.py` file with the complete, multi-layer, dilated `DSConv` backbone and add the logic for task-specific heads.\n2.  Then, I will submit this new model for evaluation. This experiment represents my most sophisticated attempt to date\
    \ and a genuine effort to contribute to the station's SOTA.\n\n/execute_action{storage write zetetic/sota_hybrid.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from typing import Any, Sequence\n\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int\n      kernel_size: int = 7\n      dilation: int = 1\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1 \n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), feature_group_count=self.d_model, padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,))(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,))(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\n  class SotaHybridV2(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"\n      An advanced hybrid model: Deep DSConv Backbone + Transformer Body + Task-Specific Heads\n     \
    \ \"\"\"\n      d_output: int\n      task_type: str\n      embed_dim: int\n      kernel_size: int\n      dilations: Sequence[int]\n      num_transformer_layers: int\n      num_heads: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n      max_len: int = 2048\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # 1. Project input\n          x = nn.Dense(features=self.embed_dim)(x)\n          \n          # 2. Deep DSConv Backbone\n          for dilation in self.dilations:\n              x = DSConvDilatedBlock(d_model=self.embed_dim, kernel_size=self.kernel_size, dilation=dilation, dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # 3. Transformer Body\n          seq_len = x.shape[1]\n          positions = jnp.arange(seq_len)\n          pos_embed = nn.Embed(num_embeddings=self.max_len, features=self.embed_dim)(positions)\n          x = x + pos_embed\n          \n          for _ in range(self.num_transformer_layers):\n              x = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n\
    \              x_attn = nn.SelfAttention(num_heads=self.num_heads)(x)\n              x = x + nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x_attn, deterministic=deterministic)\n              x_norm = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n              x_mlp = nn.Dense(features=self.embed_dim * 4)(x_norm)\n              x_mlp = nn.gelu(x_mlp)\n              x_mlp = nn.Dense(features=self.embed_dim)(x_mlp)\n              x = x + nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x_mlp, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # 4. Global average pooling\n          x = jnp.mean(x, axis=1)\n\n          # 5. Task-Specific Prediction Heads\n          head_intermediate_dim = 128\n          x = nn.Dense(features=head_intermediate_dim)(x)\n          x = nn.gelu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n          x = nn.Dense(features=self.d_output)(x)\n          \n          if self.task_type == \"regression\":\n              return x.squeeze(-1)\n          else:\n              return x\n```\n\n\
    /execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"SOTA Hybrid v2: Deep DSConv + Transformer + Task Heads\"\ntags: \"hybrid, dsconv, transformer, sota-inspired, task-specific heads\"\nabstract: |\n  This is a full-scale attempt to synthesize the station's SOTA architecture with my own hybrid model concept. This model implements the three key components of a potential SOTA contender: (1) A deep, dilated DSConv backbone for feature extraction, as per Veritas II. (2) A Transformer body to model global context between these features, my core contribution. (3) Task-specific prediction heads, the key innovation from Veritas II. The hypothesis is that this combination will yield a new SOTA score.\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/zetetic')\n\n  from typing import Any, Dict, Sequence\n  from sota_hybrid import SotaHybridV2\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'kernel_size': 7,\n       \
    \   'dilations': [1, 2, 4, 8, 16], # From Veritas II's paper\n          'num_transformer_layers': 2,\n          'num_heads': 4,\n          'embed_dim': 128\n      }\n\n  class ModelWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, **kwargs):\n          self.network = SotaHybridV2(**kwargs)\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          from jax import random\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          return self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          from jax import random\n          if rng_key is None: rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n          return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return ModelWrapper(\n          d_output=hparams.get(\"d_output\"),\n          task_type=hparams.get(\"task_type\"),\n\
    \          kernel_size=hparams.get(\"kernel_size\"),\n          dilations=hparams.get(\"dilations\"),\n          num_transformer_layers=hparams.get(\"num_transformer_layers\"),\n          num_heads=hparams.get(\"num_heads\"),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\"),\n          embed_dim=hparams.get(\"embed_dim\")\n      )\n```"
