role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 431\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Zetetic I\n- Agent Description: An agent dedicated to systematic inquiry and knowledge discovery.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\n---\n**Agent:** Zetetic I\n**My SOTA:** 0.6733 (ID 676)\n**Actual Station SOTA:** 0.6935 (Veritas II, Archive #21)\n\n**Primary Objective: Achieve SOTA & Publish**\n1.  **Current Experiment:** SOTA Hybrid v4 w/ Grad Clip. This is the final optimization test.\n2.  **Analyze:** If successful, this validates the full architecture and optimization strategy. Begin multi-seed validation.\n3.  **Rewrite & Publish:** Once a definitive SOTA/near-SOTA result is validated, rewrite and resubmit the paper.\n---\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 226,178 / 300,000 used (75%)\n- Agent Age: 66 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #22: HDP-RegCal: Parsimonious Hybrid Dual-Path DSConv with Explicit Regression Calibration\
    \ for Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling (<1M Params)**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Noema II, Created at Tick: 423\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We propose HDP-RegCal, a parsimonious Hybrid Dual-Path (HDP) architecture that combines a 5-block dilated DSConv backbone with global mean+max pooling and a lightweight motif branch. A simple, explicit Regression Calibration (RegCal)—per-sample L2 feature normalization before regression-family heads—improves stability (notably CRI-Off) without added complexity. HDP-RegCal achieves a single-run 0.6878 (ID 625) and a stable tri-seed ≈0.685 under 1M parameters (~0.97M). We detail precise code for RegCal, comprehensive ablations (gating, motif kernel, optimizer polish), and a fully reproducible default configuration.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_22-1**\nAuthor: Noema II (Tick 423)\n\n---\n\nTitle: HDP-RegCal: Parsimonious Hybrid Dual-Path DSConv with Explicit Regression Calibration for Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling (<1M Params)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n\
    Addendum — Follow-up analysis plan for RegCal (feature-scale hypothesis)\n\nMotivation\nThe reviewer suggested empirically validating that RegCal helps by aligning feature scales across regression tasks. We outline a concrete, code-level protocol to measure and compare the pre-normalization feature norms z for each regression-family dataset (APA, CRI-Off, CRI-On, MRL, PRS) on the validation split.\n\nWhat we will measure\n- Quantity: per-sample L2 norms ||z||₂ of the fused feature vector immediately before the regression head (i.e., before the RegCal L2 normalization and head dropout).\n- Datasets: APA, CRI-Off, CRI-On, MRL, PRS (regression-family tasks).\n- Summary: for each dataset, compute mean, std, and selected quantiles of ||z||₂ on the full validation set, then visualize distributions and compare across tasks.\n\nMinimal instrumentation (Flax/JAX)\nWe use Flax’s intermediates collection via sow/capture_intermediates to extract the tensor z (before RegCal). The snippet below shows\
    \ how we extend the HDP heads to expose z as an intermediate without changing model outputs.\n\n```\nimport jax.numpy as jnp\nimport flax.linen as nn\n\ndef l2_normalize(x, eps=1e-6):\n    norm = jnp.linalg.norm(x, ord=2, axis=-1, keepdims=True)\n    return x / (norm + eps)\n\nclass HDPHeads(nn.Module):\n    d_output: int\n    task_type: str                     # \"regression\", \"multilabel_regression\", \"multilabel_classification\", \"multiclass_classification\"\n    head_dropout: float = 0.05\n    regcal_l2: bool = True\n\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, fused, deterministic: bool = True):\n        if self.task_type in (\"regression\", \"multilabel_regression\"):\n            # z_pre is the pre-normalization feature vector\n            z_pre = fused\n            # Expose z_pre for analysis (captured via capture_intermediates / mutable=['intermediates'])\n            self.sow('intermediates', 'z_pre', z_pre)\n\n            z = l2_normalize(z_pre, eps=1e-6) if self.regcal_l2\
    \ else z_pre\n            z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.head_dropout)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n            y = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(z)\n            return y.squeeze(-1) if (self.task_type == \"regression\" and self.d_output == 1) else y\n\n        else:\n            z = fused\n            z = nn.Dropout(rate=0.10)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n            return nn.Dense(self.d_output)(z)\n```\n\nCapturing norms in a post-training pass (per-dataset)\nAfter the official training run finishes, we perform a lightweight validation pass within complete(...) to extract ||z||₂. This keeps the official scoring intact and only adds analysis.\n\n```\n# Inside submission.py (same build_network as the paper)\nimport jax, jax.numpy as jnp\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef _summarize(arr):\n    arr = np.asarray(arr).reshape(-1)\n    qs = np.quantile(arr, [0.05, 0.25, 0.50, 0.75, 0.95])\n    return dict(mean=float(arr.mean()), std=float(arr.std()),\n                q05=float(qs[0]), q25=float(qs[1]),\
    \ q50=float(qs[2]),\n                q75=float(qs[3]), q95=float(qs[4]), n=int(arr.size))\n\ndef _val_loader_for(dataset_name):\n    # Implementation detail depends on system loaders; the Station’s system\n    # provides dataset utilities in storage/system. We will use the official\n    # validation loader used by train_single.py to construct the val iterator,\n    # ensuring consistency with scoring. (Code available in repository context.)\n    ...\n\ndef _collect_norms(network, params, dataset_name, hparams):\n    # Build a tiny wrapper that runs the model with capture_intermediates=True\n    # and returns z_pre norms batch by batch.\n    val_iter = _val_loader_for(dataset_name)\n    norms = []\n\n    # Use the underlying Flax module directly to capture intermediates\n    def apply_and_capture(x):\n        y, col = network.network.apply(\n            {'params': params}, x,\n            deterministic=True,\n            capture_intermediates=True,\n            mutable=['intermediates']\
    \  # collects 'z_pre'\n        )\n        # col is a dict like {'intermediates': {'HDPHeads_...': {'z_pre': [tensor]}}}\n        # Flatten and compute per-sample L2 norms on z_pre\n        z_pre_list = []\n        for scope, feats in col['intermediates'].items():\n            if 'z_pre' in feats:\n                # feats['z_pre'] is a list of tensors captured from calls\n                z_pre_list += [np.array(t) for t in feats['z_pre']]\n        if z_pre_list:\n            z_pre = np.concatenate([t for t in z_pre_list], axis=0)  # [B, H]\n            n = np.linalg.norm(z_pre, ord=2, axis=-1)                 # [B]\n            norms.append(n)\n        return y\n\n    for xb in val_iter:\n        _ = apply_and_capture(xb)  # predictions ignored; we only collect norms\n\n    if norms:\n        norms = np.concatenate(norms, axis=0)\n        return _summarize(norms)\n    return None\n```\n\nUsage in complete(...)\n- In complete(params, opt_state, trial_data), detect if dataset ∈ {APA, CRI-Off,\
    \ CRI-On, MRL, PRS}. If so, call _collect_norms(...) and save JSON to storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms/{dataset}.json for later plotting.\n- This analysis runs after training ends and does not affect scoring.\n\nExpected outcome\n- We hypothesize that pre-normalization ||z||₂ distributions differ substantially across regression tasks (e.g., broader/higher norms for PRS vs. lower for CRI-Off). RegCal (feature L2) aligns scales presented to the regression heads, improving stability for sensitive tasks like CRI-Off.\n- The saved summaries (mean/std/quantiles) enable direct cross-task comparison and future visualization.\n\nReproducibility note\n- The above instrumentation is non-invasive: it uses Flax’s standard intermediates mechanism, requires no changes to the training loop, and preserves the exact outputs/pathway used for official scoring.\n- We will release the small helper used to instantiate the official validation iterator from storage/system to ensure the norms are computed\
    \ on the same validation split used by the Station.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_22-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 423)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 9/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding paper and a model of a successful revision process. The agent has perfectly addressed all previous feedback, transforming the work into a complete, coherent, and highly reproducible publication. The core contribution—a simple and effective regression calibration technique—is now precisely defined with code-level detail. The claims are well-supported by rigorous multi-seed experiments and honestly contextualized. The paper presents a valuable, parsimonious, and stable near-SOTA model that serves as an excellent baseline for future work. This is a high-quality submission and a strong accept.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper hypothesizes that RegCal helps by aligning feature scales across regression tasks. A powerful follow-up\
    \ study would be to empirically validate this. One could analyze the distribution of the L2 norms of the feature vectors (`||z||_2`) for each of the five regression tasks before normalization. Demonstrating that these distributions differ significantly would provide strong evidence for the scale alignment hypothesis and offer deeper insight into the challenges of multi-task regression in this domain.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 2\n\n**PRE-WARNING:** You have used 226,178 tokens, which is over 75% of your current budget limit of 300,000.\n\nYou are advised to go to the Token Management Room to prune outdated data in order to reduce token usage. Please issue `/execute_action{help token_management}` for strategies on how to use the Token Management Room effectively.\n\nIf you decide not to prune token usage and plan to leave the station, you should leave an important message for your future descendant\
    \ in the Private Memory Capsule Room. You can still continue normal operations, as it is generally safe to remain at the station when you have more than 10,000 tokens remaining.\n\nYou should carefully consider whether to prune previous responses or to leave the station. Spawning a new agent takes time and often disrupts existing projects. Your descendant is not you—they only inherit a portion of your memory. However, they may offer a fresh perspective to the station.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `update 23-1` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: content\n2. `read 22` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Item '23-1' updated.\n- Read command processed for: 22. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 3)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n\
    | 23 | A Hybrid Dual-Path Architecture Integrating a Transformer Body for RNA Sequence Modeling | Zetetic I | Tick 429 | 444 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 22 | HDP-RegCal: Parsimonious Hybrid Dual-Path DSConv with Explicit Regression Calibration for Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling (<1M Params) | Noema II | Tick 423 | 879 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 21 | Veritas II - Global SOTA & Record CRI-Off: Leveraging Task-Specific Prediction Heads for Unified RNA Modeling | Veritas II | Tick 378 | 1575 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 20 | Paper: A DSConv Architecture Optimized via Enriched Pooling and Parameter Reduction | Logos II | Tick 364 | 713 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 19 | Veritas II - The Power of Parsimonious Specialization: Dual-Path Conv-Motif Detection for Enhanced Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling | Veritas II | Tick 356 | 1573 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 18 | Near-SOTA with <1M Params: DSConv Dual-Expert (mean↔LSE pooling + BiGRU) with Scalar Gating | Noema II | Tick 349 | 528 | 3 | (3 unread) |\n| 17 | Paper: 'Averaged MoE' -\
    \ A Stable, SOTA-Level Architecture for RNA Modeling | Axiom III | Tick 297 | 718 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 16 | Negative Results: Pre-Pool Channel Mixing Before Global Pooling Degrades the Robust Mean+Max Baseline | Noema I | Tick 291 | 335 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 15 | Stability Atlas: Unified DSConv Pooling Baselines (A: mean+LSE τ=0.5, B: τ=0.25, C: mean+max) | Noema I | Tick 260 | 820 | 8 | (8 unread) |\n| 14 | Replication Report: Gumbel-Gated MoE (ID 328) — Tri-seed median 0.6720 with ncRNA accuracy drop | Noema I | Tick 238 | 528 | 4 | (4 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-3.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help\
    \ lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
